# `just` carries four meanings in technical prose: `only` (`reads just
# the header`), `simply` (`the hook just skips the file`), `exactly`
# (`just before the write`), and `a moment ago` (`the file was just
# created`). The reader picks one from context, and the word adds
# nothing in the `simply` sense. The fix writes `only` where the word
# limits, `immediately` where it times an event, and deletes it
# everywhere else. The rule reports every sense, including the two that
# the minimizer rule passes (`just one entry`, `the file was just
# created`). The second also reads as `was only created`, so deleting
# the word or naming the event it follows makes the sentence read one
# way.
#
# The minimizer rule reports `just` before an imperative verb, a verb
# that gives a command (`just run make`), so the guard after the word
# skips those verbs. That guard also skips `just as`, `just like`, and
# `just in case`, fixed phrases that no fix suits (`skips aliases, just
# as the parser does`). After a negation or `than`, the word carries a
# contrast (`you cannot just delete the lock`, `more than just a
# cache`), so the guard before it skips those words. The not-only rule
# reports `not just` and `more than just`. Weir has no lookbehind, so
# the filter consumes the word before `just` and narrows the finding
# back to it. The second branch catches `just` before punctuation, where
# the first branch finds no space after the word. A sentence that opens
# with `Just` passes.
#
# It hit 280 lines in the measure.py corpus, and in 20 of 20 sampled
# hits, `only`, `immediately`, or deleting the word read as clearly or
# better. The gain for each hit is small, so the rule is a suggestion.
expr neg [not, never, isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't, can't, cannot, couldn't, won't, doesn't, don't, didn't, shouldn't, than]
expr verbs [run, use, add, call, set, pass, type, install, copy, edit, change, create, delete, remove, update, point, drop, put, write, open, click]
expr main [<(!@neg just ![@verbs, as, like, (in case)]), just>, <(!@neg just!( )), just>]
let message "`just`. Write `only` where it limits, `immediately` where it times, or delete it."
let description "Flags `just`, which reads as `only`, `simply`, `exactly`, or `a moment ago`."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The parser reads only the header."
allows "You cannot just delete the lock."
allows "The hook skips the file."
allows "The walker skips aliases, just as the parser does."
allows "Check the lock again, just in case."
